  "content": {
    "id": "p33-q15-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "15",
    "prompt": "Consider $(1+x)^3(1+x)^4=(1+x)^7$. Show that\n$$\\binom{3}{0}\\binom{4}{3}+\\binom{3}{1}\\binom{4}{2}+\\binom{3}{2}\\binom{4}{1}+\\binom{3}{3}\\binom{4}{0}=\\binom{7}{3}.$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\displaystyle \\binom{3}{0}\\binom{4}{3}+\\binom{3}{1}\\binom{4}{2}+\\binom{3}{2}\\binom{4}{1}+\\binom{3}{3}\\binom{4}{0}=\\binom{7}{3}$.\n\nCompare coefficients of $x^3$.",
      "worked": "In the product $(1+x)^3(1+x)^4$, the $x^3$ terms come from $1\\times x^3$, $x\\times x^2$, $x^2\\times x$ and $x^3\\times1$. Their coefficient is\n$$\\binom{3}{0}\\binom{4}{3}+\\binom{3}{1}\\binom{4}{2}+\\binom{3}{2}\\binom{4}{1}+\\binom{3}{3}\\binom{4}{0}.$$\nIn $(1+x)^7$, the coefficient of $x^3$ is $\\binom{7}{3}$. Equating coefficients gives\n$$\\begin{align*}\\binom{3}{0}\\binom{4}{3}+\\binom{3}{1}\\binom{4}{2}+\\binom{3}{2}\\binom{4}{1}+\\binom{3}{3}\\binom{4}{0}&=\\binom{7}{3}.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
